# syntax=ghcr.io/hyper-light/shards
#
# A shards microVM that applies a slates VFS over shards' NATIVE virtio-fs backend (§4.6, the slates integration).
# The guest mounts a slates volume by its virtio-fs tag `shards0` — no kernel NFS, no host mount, no 9p. The slates
# daemon is the backend (A-161): it serves one attachment on a stream end in the same le32-length + FUSE framing
# shards' fs device speaks, and `slates shards ID --fd N -- <spawner>` hands that end to the VM's spawner.
#
# DAX compatibility: the slates side IS DAX-capable (the device DAX window — FUSE SETUPMAPPING over a pinned
# arena block — is built and hardened; see ../../wip/SLATES_DESIGN.md §4.6 and the [[dax-mapping-gate]] memory). The
# shards microVM gets the NON-DAX FUSE path here, because shards' fs device has no guest-physical DAX cache window
# yet; when it gains one, the same backend serves SETUPMAPPING and the guest reaches the block through its own page
# table. So "DAX-compatible" = the backend supports DAX; the microVM is accelerated once shards adds the window.
#
# STATUS (revised 2026-10-21): the `SLATES` line below is a WITHDRAWN proposal, kept so the history reads. It is
# not a shards directive, and its shape was wrong: it puts a host socket path into a signed Agentfile, where per-run
# state does not belong. The revised shape (README.md, "Owed follow-ups") splits the DECLARATION — the Agentfile says
# where the agent mounts a slates volume and whether it may write, in shards' syntax — from the BINDING, done at
# launch: the runner asks the slates daemon for a connected descriptor for ONE attachment and passes it as
# `--slates-fs-fd`. As of 2026-10-24: `--slates-fs-fd` exists only on shards branch `slates-fs-native`, which is on
# origin but not in `gpu`; the slates daemon serves the route and `slates shards` is the binding (A-161); the one-file
# example server is gone.

FROM alpine:3.22

# WITHDRAWN proposal (see STATUS above): the socket does not belong here; the binding is a launch-time descriptor.
SLATES ${SLATES_SOCK} shards0 /slates

# Hand off to the workload once the slates volume is mounted.
ENTRYPOINT ["/bin/sh", "-c", "mount -t virtiofs shards0 /slates && exec \"$@\"", "--"]
CMD ["cat", "/slates/hello"]
